course: english
lesson: 5
title: Handwriting: holding the pencil and joining up
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A pencil held in a fist like a dagger is driven by the big muscles of the arm, so it wobbles and the hand tires after one line. Schools teach the tripod grip: the pencil pinched between thumb and first finger, resting on the middle finger, near the tip. The small finger muscles can then steer it.
explain: Every letter has a starting point and a direction. Isla starts her «d» at the bottom and pushes up; her teacher says «start at the top». Many letters, a, d, g, o and q, begin just like c, so practising a good c helps them all. Letters started the right way come out even and are much easier to join later.
explain: Letters come in three sizes. Small ones like a, c, e sit between the lines; tall ones like b, d, h reach up (ascenders); tails like g, p, y hang below the line (descenders). Capitals belong only at the start of sentences and names, not in the middle of words, and a finger space between words lets the reader find each word: «Thecatsatonthemat» is hard work.
explain: Mixing up b and d is very common up to about seven, because they are mirror images and the brain is still learning that direction matters for letters. A trick: make two fists with thumbs up, b on the left, d on the right, and you have a bed. Left-handers write just as well as right-handers; they tilt the paper the other way and sit where their elbow won't bump a neighbour's.
explain: Handwriting still matters: in tests a marker can only mark what they can read, and joined-up writing (cursive) helps speed and even spelling, because the hand learns letter groups like «ing» and «th». But neat letters and good writing are different skills: a brilliant story can come in messy letters, and the two are marked separately.
fact: The tripod grip holds the pencil between thumb and first finger, resting on the middle finger; a fist grip tires the hand fast.
fact: Each letter has a correct starting point and direction; most small letters start at the top.
fact: The letters a, d, g, o and q start like c.
fact: Tall parts of letters (b, d, h) are ascenders; tails below the line (g, p, y) are descenders; the line letters sit on is the baseline.
fact: Mixing up b and d is common up to about age seven and does not by itself mean dyslexia.
fact: Joined-up writing is called cursive; joining helps speed, and practising joins in groups like «ing» and «th» helps spelling.
fact: Left-handed writers tilt the paper to the right and sit on the left of a right-handed partner; forcing them to use the right hand, as was done in the past, caused harm.
fact: Legible means clear enough to read; in a test, writing the marker cannot read cannot get a mark.
fact: Capital letters go at the start of sentences and names only, never in the middle of words.
fact: A finger space is a gap about one finger wide between words.
fact: Handwriting (presentation) and composition (what is written and how well) are different skills and are marked separately.
fact: Slang in this lesson: «innit» is a London and South-East tag meaning «isn't it» or «right?»; «lush» is Welsh slang for lovely and «cariad» is Welsh for darling.
exercise: Alfie holds his pencil in his fist like a dagger. Why does his hand tire? | Big arm muscles do the work and are hard to control | The pencil is too sharp | He presses far too lightly on the page to make a proper dark mark | His pencil is the wrong colour
exercise: What is the tripod grip? | Pencil between thumb and first finger, resting on the middle one | Pencil held in a tight fist with all five fingers wrapped round it, like a dagger | Pencil held flat in the palm of the hand | Pencil held between two fingers like chopsticks
exercise: Isla starts her «d» at the bottom. What does her teacher say? | Start at the top | Start in the middle of the page | Start from the right-hand side | Start with the tail
exercise: Which letters start like c? | a, d, g, o, q | b, h, k, l, t | i, j, l, t | m, n, r, u, w
exercise: Ben mixes up b and d at six. What is most likely? | It is a very common stage at his age | He must be dyslexic | He needs glasses straight away, before it gets worse | He is not trying hard enough to look at the letters
exercise: What is the «bed» trick for? | Telling b and d apart | Going to sleep early at night | Joining up the letters in a word like «bed» | Holding the pencil the right way
exercise: Kayla's dad says «no-one writes by hand any more, innit». What does «innit» mean here? | Isn't it, or right? | In it, inside something | Do it right now, quickly | I really don't know, mate
exercise: Why does joined-up writing help? | It speeds writing up and the hand learns letter groups | It makes the page look more grown-up | Joined letters use less ink | Teachers find it easier to spot and mark the wrong answers in a test
exercise: Jack's huge letters wander all over the line. What helps? | Paper with guide lines for small, tall and tail letters | A thicker pencil and bigger paper | Writing faster so the letters don't wander | Being told to write «neater» again and again, without being shown how
exercise: What is a descender? | The tail of a letter below the line | The tall part of a letter above the others | The line letters sit on | The dot on top of an i or a j
exercise: Freya is left-handed and smudges her work. What helps? | Tilting her paper to the right | Making her use her right hand instead | Writing in a gel pen, not pencil | Writing only on the left of the page
exercise: Leo writes so fast in a test that no-one can read it. What happens? | Unreadable answers can't get marks | The marker guesses what he meant | He gets extra marks for speed | The test is marked by a computer that can read anything
exercise: Gran in Cardiff calls the writing «lush». What does «lush» mean? | Lovely | Very messy | Still wet | Too big
exercise: Where do capital letters belong? | At the start of sentences and names | Anywhere they look nice | In the middle of long words, to show where the hard part is | At the end of every line
exercise: Nate writes «Thecatsatonthemat». What is missing? | Spaces between words | Capital letters on every word | A full stop in the middle | Joined-up letters in each word
exercise: A mum says messy writing means bad marks for a story. What is true? | Handwriting and composition are marked separately | Messy writing always means a bad story | Neat writing always gets full marks | Teachers never look at handwriting at all, only at spelling and full stops
test: «lefties write wrong, my nan says they made 'em use their right hand back in the day». What is true? | Left-handers write just as well; forcing the right hand caused harm | Left-handers really do write wrongly | Forcing the right hand was a good idea that helped | Left-handers can only ever write capital letters, so they should practise with the right hand
test: «ugh my hand's dead after one line, I hold it like a sword». What would you tell him? | Pinch it near the tip with thumb and finger and rest it on the middle finger | Grip harder with the whole fist so it doesn't slip | Use both hands at once to share the work, one pushing and one steering the pencil | Hold the very end of the pencil, far from the tip
test: «b and d again!! is something wrong with him?» Ben's mum is worried. What is the calm answer? | It's common up to about seven; try the «bed» trick | Yes, it always means a serious problem | Yes, and he should stop writing for a year | No, it means he's very clever
test: What would happen if Nate kept leaving no spaces between words? | Readers would struggle to find where words begin and end | Nothing, readers can always see where words end | His writing would be read twice as fast | His spelling marks would go up, because no spaces means no mistakes between words
test: «write faster, bruv, the marker'll work out what you meant». Is that right? | No, a marker can only mark what they can read | Yes, markers always guess the best meaning | Yes, speed always matters more than anything | No, markers never read the answers
test: A cartoon pencil shows «a» starting at the bottom and going up. What should it show? | Start like a c at the top, then the down stroke | Start at the bottom on the line, then go up and round the other way, like a backwards c | Start with a dot in the middle | Start from the right and go left
test: «lush that, cariad, but why've you got capitals in the middle?» What should the child fix? | Use capitals only at the start of sentences and names | Make every letter a capital | Put capitals at the end of words | Remove all capital letters, even at the start
test: «no-one writes by hand any more, it's all phones». Why might joining up still help? | The hand learns letter groups, which helps spelling and speed | Phones will be banned soon, so handwriting is the only way | Joining is only for tidiness and does nothing else | Joined letters are easier for computers and phones to read when they scan a page
test: «her writing's ugly so her story's rubbish». What is the fair answer? | A great story can be in messy writing; they're different skills | Messy writing means a bad story every time | Neat writing makes any story better, whatever the words say | Stories are only marked on neatness
test: ★★★ Which word has BOTH an ascender and a descender? | dog | sun | cat | man
test: ★★★ How many letters in «happy» hang below the line? | 3: p, p and y | 1: just y | 2: p and p | 0: none do
test: ★★★ In the middle of a sentence, Jo writes «mydoG». How many fixes does it need? | 2 | 1 | 3 | 4
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the tripod grip? | The pencil is held between the thumb and forefinger and supported by the middle finger | The pencil is grasped firmly in the closed fist and propelled across the page by the muscles of the arm and shoulder | The pencil is balanced upon the palm and guided by the wrist | The pencil is held between the third and fourth fingers
exam: Identify the principal reason for which illegible handwriting is disadvantageous in a written examination. | An answer which the examiner cannot read cannot be awarded marks | Illegible handwriting is invariably penalised with a fine | The examiner is obliged to award full marks regardless | Illegible handwriting causes the paper to be destroyed
exam: It is given that a pupil of six years frequently reverses the letters b and d. Select the response consistent with the established facts. | Such reversals are common at that age and do not of themselves indicate dyslexia | Such reversals conclusively demonstrate the presence of dyslexia | Such reversals indicate a defect of eyesight requiring immediate treatment | Such reversals are unknown among pupils of that age
exam: Ascertain the relation which subsists between handwriting and composition in the assessment of pupils' writing. | They are distinct skills which are assessed separately | They constitute one skill and are assessed together | Composition is assessed solely by reference to handwriting | Handwriting is not assessed in any circumstances
